rapid increase in coronavirus cases (COVID-19) in the European Union/European Economous Economic Zone and the United Kingdom from January 1st to 15th March 2020
The growing events of coronavirus disease (COVID-19) show a similar trend between European Union/European Economic Zone countries and the United Kingdom that ensures that, even if it is at a different stage depending on the country, the COVID-19 pandemic is advancing rapidly in all countries.
Baiding the knowledge received from Italy, patients suffering from COVID-19 will have to step up preparations for the rapid growth of patients who will need health care and especially need intensive care.
A sworm case of unidentified theory of unidentified pneumonia was reported in Wuhan, Hubei province, China, on December 31, 2019.
On January 9, 2020, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention in Chin, announced that the novel-corrupted coronavirus as an effective ingredient is now referred to as severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2).
Therefore, SARS-CoV-2 infection was named as a result of coronavirus disease (COVID-19).
Autumnical data so day that 80% of people suffering from COVID-19 have mild diseases, i.e. respiratory duct infections and most of them, including or without pneumonia.
In 14% of cases, COVID-19 becomes a more serious disease that creates the need for hospitalization, while 6% face serious illnesses that require intensive care.
The mortality rate for patients hospitalized due to COVID-19 is about 4%.
In this survey, we are reviewing the trend of every European Union/European Economic Regional (EU/EEA) and the United Kingdom's (UK) (UK), and compared them to China's Hubei province.
In addition, we compare the current number of COVID-19 cases in the EU/EEA countries and in the United U.S. January 31st - 15th March 2020.
COVID-19 events in the EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom
China's backward COVID-19 spread more geographically, and the dynamics of the COVID-19 pandemic in the rest of the world are currently following this country.
The World Health Organization (HWHO) Director-General declared COVID-19 as a pandemic on March 11, 2020.
According to Euroservance March 5th, Spitery and its companions have reported the first European confirmed COVID-19 cases as the WHO case definition.
At the EU/EEA, the first three confirmed cases were reported by France on January 24, 2020.
As of March 15th 2020, all 30 EU/EEA cases were detected in the United Kingdom (UAK) COVID-19, where 39,768 cases and 1,727 deaths were reported from 31st December 2019, where there have been 17,750 cases and 1,441 deaths only in Italy.
Receivable Numbers of COVID-19 Cases and Responsible Range
The European Centers for Disease Prevention (ECDDC) only communicates about COVID-19 cases calculated in every country globally acquired from government sources such as the Ministry of Health, the National and Regional Health Authority of the United Nations, which is updated every day at 8:00 a.m.
This data was used to assess trends in the EU/EEA and the UK's COVID-19, and to compare what happened in Italy.
As a proxy for the outbreak of active COVID-19 cases, we calculated a 14-day short-growing events of COVID-19, thus considering the natural trajectory of COVID-19 in each of the EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom on January 15, 2020.
We have also presented the cumulative numbers of recorded cases in each country until 8:00 p.m. on March 15, 2020 and has been compared to Italian cases during the 31st January-15th March 2020.
COVID-19 trends in EU/EEA Countries and the United Kingdom
In the EU/EEA countries and in the United EIA countries and in the United Quantity (ICU), the trend of Hubei Province (China) generally followed the trend.
For the EU/EEA and the U.K. overall, the growing incidence of COVID-19 began to grow in the vicinity on February 21st and then sharply grew in the vicinity of 28th February 2020 (complementary ingredients).
It was mainly driven by a rapid increase in the number of cases reported in Italy, but all other EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom have shown a similar trend of increasing surge of COVID-19 (comprehensive materials).
Figure 2 compares Italy's numbers in the EU/EEA countries for the period January 1-15 March 2020 and the number of COVID-19 cases in the United Kingdom.
It's worth noting that, until 8:00 p.m. on March 15, 15 other EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom have already already reported comparable cases to Italy only before or les than 3 weeks.
Our results indicate that the number of known cases of COVID-19 is growing rapidly in the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom.
The trend noticed in the cumulative events of COVID-19 indicates that the pandemic is progressing at a relatively pasce in all countries.
Despite the fact that countries are at different levels, despite differences in national public muvency responses, and despite the different definitions of those affected in countries and various protocols for the selection of patients that must be tested for confirmation of COVID-19, including catch-up testing.
Earlier in March 2020, doctors in the affected areas of Italy described a situation where 10% of patients infected with COVID-19 needed intensive care (intensive care) and media sources reported that hospitals and intensive care units in these areas had already reached their maximum capacity.
The admission form of COVID-19 patients in the hospital and/or an intensive care unit is currently available only 6% and 1% at the EU/EEA level respectively (data has not been shown).
However, they should be systematically collected to meet the current surveillance information, which focuses on the number and death number of reported cases.
A survey conducted in 2010–11 in 2010–11 shows the difference between intensive care and the availability of intermediate cares and intermeditate care beds per 100,000 people in Portugal from 29.2 in Germany.
This means that countries can have fewer or more resources than Italy (2010–11 every 10.5 intensive care and intermediate care beds per 10,000.).
With the prevalence of COVID-19 cases admitted to each EU/EEA country and the UK, modeling scenarios have been found that there is a >90%-riskep risk of oversweepting the capacity of intensive care beds and it is provided in the Sixth Update of ECDC's rapid risk assessment of COVID-19.
Since the events have so full in EU/EEA countries and in some regions of the United Kingdom, hospitals and intensive care units typically provide to the public using a specific regional service, it is recommended to be available in the system of naming regional unitium for data statistic 2 (NNUTS-2) about the affected and intensive care bed.
The experience gained from Italy and current trends in other countries are showing that the COVID-19 pandemic is progressing rapidly in the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom.
So the ongoing community of SARS-CoV-2 in countries, hospitals and intensive care units should prepare themselves to increase the number of COVID-19 patients, such as what happened in the affected regions of Italy.
Just as the recent ECDC has been pointed out in the rapid risk assessment of SARS-COV-2 is vital to delay the spread of infection, with a change from suppression to mitigation, since the expected rapid increase in the number of events may not give enough time to convince the recipients and hospitals to understand, accept, and provide their responses accordingly.
Rapid risk evaluation also lists public health-related measures to alleviate the impact of the pandemic.
There is a short period of opportunity that countries have the potential to further increase their control efforts to reduce the spread of SARS-CoV-2 and reduce the pressure on healthcare.
Unwreating to do so, there is a possibility that healthcare systems in other EU/EEA countries will face an escalation to see an increase in the number of patients who need intensive care over the next few days or weeks.
