Plasma and antibodies produced by patients
There has a long history of collecting blood from patients recovering from infectious diseases to treat other patients suffering from the same disease or to protect healthy people from falling into the clutches of the disease.
In fact, recovering patients often have relatively high levels of antibodies in terms of pathogens in their blood.
Antibodies are an immunoglobulin (Ig) produced by B lymphocytes to fight germs and other external objects, and they detect distinct molecules of pathogens and mitigate them directly.
Based on this, plasma was collected from the blood of a group of patients recovering from COVID-19 and injected into 10 critically ill patients.
Their symptoms were improved within 24 hours, and there was a decrease in inflammation and viral load with him, and the insurgence of oxygen in the blood was improved.
However, verification and clarification is necessary to propose procedures for large-sufficienous uses not yet yet. However, verification and clarification is necessary.
Also, in view of the medical impact, some of the disadvantages associated with Plasma should be carefully considered.
Antibodies, for example, can exaggerate the immune response and cause cytokine release syndrome, which is a potential lethal toxicity.
The concentration of antibodies in the blood is generally low, and there is a huge demand for plasma to treat critically sick patients.
It's inconceivable to quickly develop and develop antibodies at certain stages to fight the global pandemic.
Thus, it is more important and fruitful to disassemble B cells from patients and detect genetic codes to monitor or encoding effective antibodies against the necessary proteins of the virus or encoding effective antibodies.
In this way, we can easily increase the production of antibodies.
