The coronavirus (VCoV-s) belongs the family, with a group wrapped, positive-sensitive, single-stranded RNA virus.
“Sharing the largest genom of RNA viruses from 26 to 32 kilobebes, these viruses were termed “‘CoV” due to the crown-like morphology under electron microscopes.””
Structurally, CoV has an inseparable genome, which is the same organization.
Approximate two-thirds of the genomes have two large overlapping open reading frames (ORF1a and ORF1b), which are converted into pp1a and pp1ab replicase polyprotine (translation).
Polyproteins are processed to produce 16 more non-structural proteins, named nsp1~16.
The rest of the genom contains ORF for constructive proteins that include wetches (S), membranes (E), membranes (M) and nucleoproteins (N).
A number of line-specific accessory proteins are also encodized by different lines of CoVs.
Based on the differences between protein sequences, CoVs are classified into four masses (Alpha-Covi, Beta-Covi, Gama-Covi and Delta-Covi), which contains most of the HCoV in the beta-CoV mass and it is divided into four breeds (A, B, C and D).
Ethnic evidence has shown that bats and rats act as gene sources of most alpha-CoV and beta-CoVs, while birds are the main bases of gamma-CoV and delta-CoV.
For thousands of years, VCoVs have continuously overcome interspecial barriers and emerged as a number of major human pathogens.
So far, there have been identified seven human CoVs.
HCoV-229E and HCoV-NL63 are alpha-CoV.
The other five beta-CoV include HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1, civiar Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus (SARS-CoV), Middle East Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus (MERS-CoV) and SARS-CoV-2.
HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 and HCoV-NL63 usually cause mild symptoms such as common colds and/or diarrhea.
On the other hand, SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV and newly detected SARS-VCoV-2 are highly pathogenic, acute respiratory infection symptoms (Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome, ARDS) and are highly likely to manifest outside the lungs.
The first HCoV-229E strain was collected from the discharge of the nose in patients with common colds in the mid-1960's B814 1960s.
