The first HCoV-229E strain was separated from the respiratory tract in 1966 to patients suffering from upper respiratory infections, and was subsequently adapted to the WI-38 lung cell line.
Patients infected with HCoV-229E showed common cold symptoms, including headache, sneezing, illness, and sore throat, while 10~20% of cases of fever and cough were seen.
Later in 1967, HCoV-OC43 was collected from organ cultural culture and subsequent repetrial growth of the virus in the brain of dairy mice.
Clinical characteristics of HCoV-OC43 infection are similar to symptoms caused by HCoV-229E, which cannot be symptomatically separated from infections of other respiratory duct pathogens such as influenza A virus and rhinovirus.
HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43 are both spread worldwide and are mainly infected in the winter climate during the temperate season.
Usually, the inauguration of these two viruses is less than a week, followed by an illness of about 2 weeks.
According to a study by Human Volunteers, healthy people with HCoV-229E have developed a mild common cold.
