Due to the recent outbreak of SARS-CoV-2, CoVs have once again returned to the center of discussion.
The study of CoVs among bats and other animals has significantly changed our perception of animal sources in human infections and the importance of HCoV's animal harbor.
Extensive evidence has proven that SARS-VCoV, MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV-COV-2 have a bat source and they are transmitted to humans through intermediary.
Since the SARS-VCoV infection stems from contact between humans and odors in the market, shutting the wet make market and killing the odors there could effectively put an end to the SARS pandemic.
Through the same argument, pangolins should be removed from wet markets to prevent faculous transmission in view of the discovery of multiple tribes of Pangolin Beta-Co-CoV, closely related to SARS-VCoV.
However, it still remains to be clarified in future investigations on what and how SARS-VCoV-2 is transmitted to humans through forestruis and other mammals.
On the other hand, MERS-CoV has existed for a long time in a hugged camel.
These camels serve as a major source of meat, milk, leather, and fur for local people in addition to important instruments of transportation.
They spread throughout the Middle East and Africa.
So it is impossible to sacrifice all camels to control MERS, as what was done in China's wild animal market to prevent SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-COV-2.
In order to stop the repeated outpreases of MERS, a comprehensive approach should be taken to develop effective vaccines against MERS-CoV for camels in combination with other infection control systems.
The prevalence of new genotypes may arise as we are not able to eliminate these viruses.
There are many types of CoVs in nature.
In particular, bat CoV is very diverse with the potential of animals.
There is a huge potential for aggravating and re-connecting of these zoonotic CoVs, as a result of the emergence of new CoVs that will be more transmissible and/or fatal to humans in the future.
The culture of eating wild animals in some parts of in China to reduce unnecessary communication between humans and animals should be abandoned.
With SARS, MERS and COVID-19's arbitration, there should be a plan of better preparedness and feedback.
There have been a lot of virouts on the earth for a long time.
They live in their own natural turmoil until there is an opportunity to spread.
Although bats have many tratities that are in favor of spreading the virus, the opportunity to come into contact with human bats and other wildlife species can be minimized if people are aware to stay away from them.
To a better understanding of the equatives of CoVs and their natural servators, it is necessary to take continuous surveillance of mammals, which will prove effective to prevent human infections and future prevalence from animals.
In the conclusion, the most effective way to prevent animal-borne diseases in humans is to stay away from the ecological niche of the natural source of the animal virus.
Several pieces of the animal source puzzle of SARS-CoV-2 are still missing.
First of all, it is necessary to look at the situation in which bats and bats can share the same environmental niche.
Secondly, if bats play a more direct role in human transmission, it should be determined how people come into contact with bats.
Thirdly, if a third mammal acts as a real intermediate host, it is necessary to clarify how it interacts with different species, including humans, bats, and pangolins.
Finally, many mammals, including domestic animals, can be susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, both surveillance and experimental infections.
If it's bats, pangolins or any other mammals, it's expected that SARS-CoV-2 or its almost identical genitary viruses will be identified in its natural hosts in the future.
Continuous explorations in this case will explain the evolutionary pathway of SARS-CoV-2 in animals, which will have an important impact on preventing and controlling COVID-19 in humans.
