Hand washing (or handwashing), also known as hand hygiene, refers to a person's ability to clean their hands for the purpose of removing soil, greases, microscopic organisms or other unwanted substances.
“At certain “imminimable moments” during “special—day” prevent the spread of many diseases, for example diarrhea and cholera, which is transmitted through the stool-mouth path.”
People may also be infected with respiratory diseases such as influenza or a normal cold, for example if they don't wash their hands before touching their eyes, nose, or mouth (i.e. mucous membranes).
The five most important moments of the day when it is important to wash hands with soap are: before and after defecation, after cleansing the baby's downside or changing the nappy, before feeding the baby, before eating and making food or washing raw meat, fish, or poultry.
If water and soap are not available, hands can be cleaned with ashes. The World Health Organization recommends hand-washing:
Before preparing the food, during and after the preparation of the food.
before and after taking care of a sick person.
After changing diapers or cleaning a child who used the toilet.
Your noses after coughing or sneezing.
After touching animal, animal feed, or animal wastage.
The health of medical hand hygiene refers to healthy habits related to medical procedures.
Wash your hands before giving medication or taking medical care care, prevent or reduce the spread of the disease.
The mast medical purpose of hand washing is to keep away from pathogens (bacteria, viruses, or other microorganisms that can cause the disease) and to keep their hands clean from chemicals that can cause the disease.
It is especially important for people who oversee or work food in the medical field, but it is also an important procedure for ordinary people.
There are many health-related health benefits, including influenza, influenza, coronavirus and other infectious diseases; prevention of contagious causes of diarrhea; reducing respiration infection;
And reducing the infant mortality rate at home.
A 2013 study has found that the advanced habit of handwashing can lead to an increase in the length growth of children under the age of five.
In developing countries, hand-wrenching behavioral changes such as hand washing with soap can reduce the mortality rate of children related to respiratory and diarrhea-related diseases.
This simple process can reduce the mortality rate from the disease by about 50%.
The measures to encourage hand washing can reduce the phase of diarrhea by nearly a third, and this is comparable to a clean water supply in low-income areas.
Washing hands with soap is associated with a decrease of 48%. Washing hands with soap is the only most effective and cost-effective way to prevent diarrhea and acute respiratory infections (ARI).
Pneumonia, which is a major ARI, is the leading cause of death in children under the age of five. It takes the lives of an estimated 1.8 million children every year.
Diarrhea and Pneumonium are the simultanous causes for the deaths of about 3.5 million children every year.
According to UNICEF, hand-washing with soap can save more lives than a vaccine or medical interfeit, reducing death from diarrhea, and reducing a quarter of deaths from acute respiration-related infections.
Hand washing as part of water, sanitation and hygiene (WASH) programs are coordinated with other sanitation measures.
Hand washing prevents infectious dermats (Impetigo) that is transmitted through direct physical contact.
One of the little harmful effects of hand washing is that frequent hand washing can cause damage to the skin due to drying of the skin.
A 202 Danish study in 2012 found that excessive hand washing can lead to itching, skin peeling conditions that are known as hand eczema or hand dermatology, which is especially common among healthcare workers.
Washing too frequently is also seen as a symptom of excessive compulsive disorder (OCD).
The five most important times of the day to reduce the feces-mouth-related infection of the disease are: after using the bathroom (divorcing, defecation), after cleaning the baby's lower country or changing the nappy before feeding the baby, before eating and making food or handing over raw meat, fish, or poultry.
Other times when proper hand washing techniques should be applied to prevent the spread of the disease, which include cuts or wounds before and after treatment; sneezing, coughing, or after brushing the noses; after touching the animal's waste or after the animal's wasted; and after touching the garbage.
